## Corrections — apply before running

- THE DESIGNED REACTION IS SAFE STATE, NOT AN MCU RESET. The product's
  latent-point self-test (SbcDriverLocal_performFwdMonDiagnostics, the
  ESM_FWD_MON measure) lowers the threshold, provokes an FWD failure,
  expects DIGITAL_IO_CH_SAFESTATE == DIO_LOW within 100 ms, then
  RECOVERS. A reset at threshold would make that recovery impossible.
  So the PR comment in functionalWatchdogRefresh() ("watchdog fail-safe
  reset takes over") is a WRONG COMMENT, and the test's step-8 oracle
  (SCU_RSTSTAT changed) is wrong BY DESIGN, not unobservable. File both
  as findings: one against the PR comment, one against the test oracle.
- Therefore: the F2(a) branch (debug session suppressing a reset) is
  VOID for this test, and its link to H8 of the reset-type campaign is
  withdrawn. Do not run a sessionless reset test. Do not mention H8 in
  the write-up except to say the link is withdrawn.
- YOUR THREE CONTROL RUNS ARE UNVALIDATED. fwdRefreshDisable is owned by
  the self-test state machine (writes at :4985 and :5009; stops touching
  it only when fwdMonStatus == SELFTEST_PASSED, :5153). You never read
  fwdMonStatus. Until step 1 below shows SELFTEST_PASSED, "withheld the
  response for 257 s" is an assumption, not a fact. Say so in the
  write-up either way.
- The 5.5 s contactor opening is a SOFTWARE reaction (application path
  on healthy=false), separate from the hardware safe state. The two must
  be timestamped independently; do not merge them into one "reaction".
- Remaining families: F1 = mechanism works and the reaction is safe
  state (expected, by design); F3 = the wrong/withheld response never
  reaches the SPI (killed by FWDECNT moving). F2 now means only: SBC
  enters safe state but the safe-state signal is not visible/wired on
